Abstract
Iron-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with low cost and excellent photocatalytic potential are extremely attractive in the field of energy utilization and environmental remediation. In this study, a novel In2S3/MIL-100(Fe) photocatalyst was successfully synthesized by a facile solvothermal method for the first time. Several technologies (such as X-ray diffraction, scanning electron microscope, transmission electron microscope, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy) were used to characterize the as-obtained samples and demonstrate the successful combination of MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ . Experimental results showed that $18\%$ of tetracycline (TC) was adsorbed under dark condition and another $70\%$ of TC was degraded under visible-light irradiation when treating $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of TC solution ( $\mathrm{{\dot{1}0\ m g/L)}}$ with $30\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/$ MIL-100(Fe) composites. The corresponding TC removal efficiency was almost 1.9 and 1.6 times higher than that of pure MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , respectively. The mechanism investigations revealed that the heterojunction composite exhibited superior charge transfer than either MIL-100(Fe) or $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , and this caused more efficient separation of electron-hole pairs. As a result, more radicals and holes were generated in the composite, leading to better photocatalytic performance. This work highlights the powerful combination of MOFs and semiconductor, which is a promising approach to fabricate heterojunction photocatalyst for wastewater purification.
Keywords: metal-organic frameworks; indium sulfide; tetracycline; photodegradation.
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a kind of porous material with large surface areas and tunable organic linkers or metal clusters, are widely used in catalysis, molecule adsorption and separation, and porous carriers [1-3]. Among them, iron-based MOFs are extremely attractive owing to that iron is an earth abundant element with low price. To date, several iron-based MOFs such as MIL-53(Fe), MIL-68(Fe), MIL-100(Fe), MIL101(Fe), and MIL-88B(Fe) have been applied in the field of photocatalysis because of their good response to visible light and the activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to radicals (·OH) via a Fenton-like route [4-9]. For example, when utilizing the reduction ability of photocatalysis, the valuable gas hydrogen, with the high energy density (142.35 $\bf k J/\underline{{g}})$ and clean combustion product (only water vapor), could be generated [10, 11]. Moreover, iron-based MOFs have highly chemical, water stabilities and low toxicity. However, their practical application was restricted by their low efficient excitons generation and charge separation, leading to low quantum efficiency and photocatalytic activity.
To address these issues, substantial efforts were conducted in the past and couple of methods were tested such as linker decoration, combination with semiconductors, metal nanoparticles loading, decoration by reduced graphene oxide and graphene, and incorporation with other functional materials [12-18]. Of these strategies, combination with semiconductors is one of the major directions for utilizing iron-based MOFs as photocatalysts, owing to the heterojunction facilitating the efficient separation of the photoexcited electron-hole pairs [19]. Zheng and co-workers found that $\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{WO}_{6}/$ MIL-100(Fe) with the special p-n heterojunction could facilitate the photo-generated charge separation and thus enhance the photocatalytic activity for the degradation of salicylic acid, with $95.1\%$ salicylic acid being degraded in $50~\mathrm{min}$ under visible-light irradiation, which was 1.5 times higher than that degraded by MIL-100(Fe) [20]. Carbon nitride nanosheet/MIL-100(Fe) was successfully synthesized for the degradation of organic dyes and the degradation rate of $100\%$ was reached in 4 hours when treating Rhodamine B aqueous solution $\mathrm{\langle50\mg/L\rangle}$ ) after adding $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [21, 22]. These reports demonstrated that it was effective to improve the photocatalytic activity of MIL-100(Fe) by coupling with other semiconductors.
Indium sulfide $\left(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\right)$ , an n-type semiconductor with a narrow band gap $(2.0{-}2.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ , can be crystallized into three polymorphic forms, i.e., cubic structure $\left({\mathfrak{a}}{-}\mathrm{In}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{3}\right)$ ), spinel structure $\mathrm{(\beta-In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3})$ , and hexagonal structure $\left(\gamma{-}\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\right)$ [23]. $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ has been increasingly considered a potential photocatalyst due to its high photoconductivity, low toxicity, and excellent chemical and physical characteristics in recent years [24, 25]. However, like other binary sulfides (e.g., $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , and CdS), $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ also faces the high recombination rate of photoexcited electron-hole pairs, which greatly reduce the photocatalytic activity [26-28]. Hence, attempts to construct with other materials have been made. $\mathrm{Ag_{3}P O_{4}}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ composites and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ catalysts were proven to show high photocatalytic performance and structural stability, owing to the constructed Z-scheme systems [29, 30]. It was previously reported that compared with the monomers, $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\$ MIL-125(Ti) core–shell microparticle and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3},$ /UiO-66 composites enhanced adsorption performance and photocatalytic activity for the removal of TC under visible-light irradiation. However, the degradation efficiencies were not high enough [31, 32]. In addition, unlike MIL-100(Fe), both MIL-125(Ti) and UIO-66 exhibited no obvious absorption peak in the visible area. These enlighten us to raise an assumption that coupling $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ with MIL-100(Fe) to form a novel photocatalyst might restrain the disadvantages and further promote visible-light photocatalytic performance. To the best of our knowledge, such $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ /MIL-100(Fe) relevant photocatalysts have not been reported before.
The aim of this work is to assess the feasibility of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/$ MIL-100(Fe) heterojunction composites as efficient photocatalysts for the removal of TC, one of the most widely used antibiotics with high toxicity, persistence and carcinogenicity [33]. Further, TC often occurs in domestic sewage after discharged into the environment and may affect the normal operation of active sludge [34, 35]. Firstly, the morphology, composition, and structure of the as-synthesized photocatalysts were characterized and analyzed through several tools such as Xray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission electron microscope (TEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption–desorption. To comprehensively evaluate the performance of the photocatalysts, TC removal performance among the as-synthesized photocatalysts and their monomers were then compared under different photocatalyst dosages, initial TC concentrations, and pH. Based on the radicals-trapping experiments and electron spin resonance (ESR) tests, the mechanisms of how $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites enhance photocatalytic performance were explored. Finally, the potential application perspective was discussed.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
All reagents and solvents used in this study were of analytical reagent grade and received from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China).
## 2.2. Synthesis and characterization of photocatalysts
MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized by the hydrothermal method as reported previously with some modifications [36]. Briefly, $\ensuremath{14.43\ \mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe(NO_{3})_{3}}\cdot9\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and $\mathbf{5.01\textrm{g}}$ of trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ were dissolved in $36~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water before $3.24\mathrm{mL}$ of hydrofluoric acid being added. After stirring magnetically for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , the mixed solution was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and maintained at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . After natural cooling, the obtained precipitates were purified by deionized water and ethanol at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. Finally, the yielded MIL-100(Fe) products were dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ .
A facile one-pot solvothermal method was used to prepare a series of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites. Typically, a desired amount of MIL-100(Fe) powder as synthesized above was dispersed into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol with the action of sonication. Then $300.8~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}_{\mathrm{XH}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and $112.7\mathrm{mg}$ of thioacetamide $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C S N H_{2})}$ ) were dissolved into the MIL-100(Fe) dispersion in turn under continuous magnetic stirring for 30 minutes. The mixture was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . After natural cooling, the precipitates were collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol for 3 times, and dried in a vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . According to this method, composites with expected MIL-100(Fe) mass rations of $6\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ , $16\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and $26\mathrm{wt\%}$ were prepared and denoted as ISMIL-6, ISMIL-16, and ISMIL-26, respectively. For comparison, the pristine $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ was also prepared under the same conditions, excepting for the presence of MIL100(Fe). The synthesis and assembly roadmap of the photocatalysts is shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.3. Tetracycline removal experiments
The photocatalytic performances of the synthesized $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/$ MIL-100(Fe) composites were evaluated by the removal of TC in aqueous solution under visible-light irradiation. TC solution of $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ was prepared in volumetric flask and the following TC solutions used in subsequent experiments were obtained by dilution. The TC removal experiments were carried out in $200\mathrm{mL}$ glass beakers containing $100\mathrm{mL}$ of the desired TC solution and a certain amount of catalyst. In order to make the catalysts better dispersed in the solution, the mixed suspension was ultrasonic treated for 3 min immediately after the catalysts were added. A 300 W Xenon lamp with a $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter was used as the visible-light source. Before irradiation, the suspension was magnetically stirred $(1000~\mathrm{rpm})$ in the dark for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to get adsorption-desorption equilibrium between photocatalyst and TC. At certain time intervals, samples ( $3~\mathrm{mL}$ each) were collected, centrifuged, filtrated by $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PTFE syringe filters, and analyzed using a UV–vis spectrophotometer with the maximal adsorption wavelength of TC at $357\mathrm{nm}$ . Additionally, The initial concentration and adsorption equilibrium concentration of TC are denoted as $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ , respectively. C is the TC concentration at different reaction times and m is the dosage of photocatalyst.
In the literature, the dosage of photocatalyst, initial TC concentration and $\mathsf{p H}$ are the main factors associated with the removal performance of TC [37-39]. In addition, different water systems may have various pollutant concentrations and pH values. Especially in aqueous solution, owing to the three structure moieties associated with different acidic dissociation constants $(\mathrm{pKa}_{1}{=}3.30$ , $\mathrm{pKa}_{2}{=}7.68$ , $\mathrm{pKa}_{3}{=}9.69$ ), TC with multiple ionizable functional groups may exist as cation species $\mathrm{(pH}<3.3\mathrm{)}$ , zwitterion species $(3.3<\mathrm{pH}<7.68)$ , or net negatively charged ions $\mathrm{(pH>7.68)}$ ) [40-42]. The effects of these factors on TC removal performance were investigated in this study. Because ISMIL-16 exhibited the best performance for TC removal according to the preliminary results, this composite was selected in the following tests. In the experiments, the dosages of ISMIL16 ranged from 0.1 to $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ and the initial TC concentrations were varied from 10 to $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . Three pH values, i.e., 3.3, 7.4, and 10.1, were tested. The other conditions and operations were the same as described above.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of obtained samples
Fig. 2a shows the XRD patterns of MIL-100(Fe), $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3^{\prime}}$ /MIL-100(Fe) composites. The diffraction peaks of MIL-100(Fe) were similar to the results obtained previously [43], demonstrating the successful preparation of MIL-100(Fe). The as-obtained $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ showed diffraction peaks located at $2\Theta{=}16.7^{\circ},27.2^{\circ},32.4^{\circ}.$ ， and $47.5^{\circ}$ , which can be indexed to the (111), (311), (400), and (440) crystal planes of cubic $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ phase structure 1 $\mathrm{(\beta-In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3})$ ) (JCPDS 32-0456) [44]. The intensities corresponding to MIL-100(Fe) reflections in $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites can not be observed clearly, which may be ascribed to the low content and high dispersion of cubic crystalline MIL-100(Fe). Moreover, with the increase of the content of MIL-100(Fe), the intensities corresponding to MIL-100(Fe) reflections increased, indicating that the crystal structure of MIL-100(Fe) was maintained successfully after the process. The same phenomenon was reported in a previous study [45]. All the diffraction peaks of the In2S3/MIL-100(Fe) composites were ascribed to the peaks of MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , implying the successful synthesis of the composites. This also suggested that no characteristic peaks of impurities or other phases was detected, demonstrating that the obtained products were the combination of MIL100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ . The presence of MIL-100(Fe) component in the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites was also confirmed by the FT-IR spectra. As shown in Fig 2b, the FT-IR spectra of MIL-100(Fe) exhibited the clear adsorption at 3436, 1627, 1450, 1381, 759, and $710~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which is identical to the reported literature [46-48]. While, for the ISMIL-16 composites, several characteristic peaks of MIL-100(Fe) were also observed and no peaks of impurities was detected, showing the good stability of MIL-100(Fe) during the preparation of In2S3/MIL-100(Fe) composites.
Fig. 3 shows the SEM images of MIL-100(Fe), $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , and ISMIL-16, respectively. It can be clearly seen that the pure MIL-100(Fe) photocatalyst consisted of small octahedral microcrystals with an average edge length ranging from 1 to $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 3a). The synthesized $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ exhibited irregular nanoparticles with a small average size (Fig. 3b). High-magnification SEM images showed that massive $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ nanoparticles were randomly decorated on the surface of MIL-100(Fe) microcrystals (Fig. 3c), indicating the tight combination between MIL100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ . In addition, MIL-100(Fe) still exhibited regular octahedron morphology in the composites, demonstrating that the structure of MIL-100(Fe) was not destroyed in the synthesized process of composites.
