The morphologies and compositional distribution of the as-synthesized photocatalysts were further characterized by TEM and HRTEM. As shown in Fig. 4a and 4b, the pristine MIL-100(Fe) exhibited uniform granular octahedral morphology with super smooth surface, while the pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ had irregular shape with the particle size ranging from 0.1 to $0.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , which was similar to the previous studies [31, 49]. In Fig. 4c, the TEM image of ISMIL-16 microparticle also exhibited irregular shapes, due to that massive $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ nanoparticles were randomly dispersed on the surface of MIL-100(Fe) microcrystals. This indicated the formation of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}$ - 100(Fe) composites, which was well consistent with the SEM micrographs. The HRTEM images clearly indicated the resolved lattice fringes of $0.193~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (440) crystallographic planes of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ (Fig. 4e). The EDX spectrum (Fig. 4d) further confirmed the presence of In, S, Fe, C, and O elements in the composites. Such facts were also compliant to the characterization of XRD, further verifying the successful synthesis of the photocatalysts.
The chemical states of ISMIL-16 was further determined by XPS measurements. As shown in Fig. 5a, the survey scan revealed that ISMIL-16 contained Fe, C, In, S, and O elements. The Fe 2p spectrum was displayed in Fig. 5b, where two dominant peaks were found at 725.1 and $704.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , assigned to Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ , respectively [50]. The satellite peak at $712.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ was the characteristic of $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ in MIL-100(Fe) [4]. In the C
1s spectrum, three deconvoluted peaks at 288.8, 285.6 and $284.8~\mathrm{eV}$ arose from MIL-100(Fe) and external carbon contamination (Fig. 5c). In the In 3d spectrum, two peaks at 445.1 and $452.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to In $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and In $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ , respectively (Fig. 5d). The XPS spectrum of S 2p can be fitted into two peaks, i.e, S $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and S $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , which appeared at 161.5 and $162.7\mathrm{eV}$ , indicating the existence of $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ (Fig. 5e). The observed binding energy values and spin orbit separations (7.6 eV, 1.2 eV) of In and S agreed well with the reported data, indicating that the In and S were present as $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ in the sample, respectively [32, 44, 51]. And the peak of S 2p spectrum at $169.3\mathrm{eV}$ may be due to the oxidation of some of the S on the surface. In addition, the O 1s peak of the composite at $532.1~\mathrm{eV}$ was observed (Fig. 5f). All these results above confirmed the successful formation of ISMIL-16 composites.
## 3.2. Tetracycline removal performance
