Fig. 6a shows TC removal performance by different catalysts under the same initial TC concentration ( $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{0}}$ $=10\mathrm{mg/L}$ ) and same sample dosage $\mathrm{(m}=30\mathrm{mg}$ ). Obviously, the adsorption-desorption equilibrium of samples were reached in $30\mathrm{min}$ before the visible-light irradiation. Of these catalysts, MIL-100(Fe) achieved the highest adsorption performance and lowest photocatalytic activity, with the total removal efficiency of $47\%$ being achieved in the whole process. As for $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , after adsorbing $26\%$ of TC in the dark, about $29\%$ of TC was degraded under visible-light irradiation, reaching $55\%$ in total. All the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites exhibited much better TC removal performance than either pure MIL-100(Fe) or $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , though the two monomers exhibited higher TC adsorption capacities than the composites. This indicated that these $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites possessed better photocatalytic activity than the two pristine materials. Especially for ISMIL-16, although $18\%$ of TC was only adsorbed in the dark, almost $70\%$ of TC was degraded in 90 min under irradiation, reaching the total removal efficiency of $88\%$ , which was almost 1.9 and 1.6 times higher than that of pure MIL100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , respectively. In addition, the TC removal capability of ISMIL-16 was $29.3\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which was also much higher than that of either MIL-100(Fe) $\mathrm{(15.7\mg/g)}$ or $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ $\mathrm{18.3\mg/g)}$ .
Fig. 6b shows TC removal by ISMIL-16 under different dosages. With an increase of ISMIL-16 dosage, the adsorption performance increased, but TC removal efficiency was in the order of $0.4~\mathrm{g/L}>0.3~\mathrm{g/L}>0.5$ $\mathrm{g/L}>0.2~\mathrm{g/L}>0.1~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The more catalyst dosages, the more active sites, leading to better adsorption and photocatalytic activity. However, excess catalysts may block the visible-light exposure to the catalysts at the bottom, which may adversely affect the photodegradation of TC. According to our calculation, TC removal capability in these cases was in the range of 22.3 to $29.3~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , with the highest TC removal capability being achieved in $0.3~\mathrm{g/L}$ . This suggests that selecting pertinent dosages in real-world situations is important. Initial TC concentration played a significant role in the photodegradation process. The removal efficiency increased from $50\%$ to $88\%$ with the initial TC concentration decreasing from 50 to $10\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ (Fig. 6c). Further investigations showed that $\mathsf{p H}$ did not affect TC removal largely. It was found that $80\%88\%$ of TC removal efficiency was obtained when pH was controlled at acidic $\mathrm{(pH~}3.3\mathrm{)}$ , neutral $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ , or alkaline $\mathrm{(pH~10.1)}$ conditions (Fig. 6d). All these results suggested that ISMIL-16 possessed high ambient range of tolerance and could be applied in different water systems.
