## 3.3. Mechanism of how ISMIL-16 enhances photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline
The removal of TC from aqueous solution by photocatalyst mainly includes two processes: adsorption and degradation. Compared with pristine MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , ISMIL-16 possessed lower adsorption ability but much better photocatalytic degradation performance (Fig. 6a). The adsorption ability of nanomaterial is closely related to their surface structure, hence the BET surface areas were measured. Fig. S1 shows the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption isotherms of the as-synthesized photocatalysts. According to the IUPAC (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry) classification, the MIL-100(Fe) sample was of type I isotherm, indicating its typical microporous structure. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites showed a type IV isotherm with a H3 typical hysteresis loop, indicating the presence of mesoporous structure. In comparison with MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/$ /MIL-100(Fe) composites attained decreased specific surface area and total pore volume (Table S1), which may result in their lower adsorption performance (Fig. 6a) [53]. These results also revealed that the enhanced photocatalytic activity of ISMIL-16 didn’t owe to the specific surface area.
According to the current photocatalytic mechanism, the photodegradation process of pollutants can be summarized in three steps: (1) the catalysts are excited by photons to produce electron-hole pairs when the energy of photons is greater than the band-gap energy $\mathrm{(E_{g})}$ of catalyst; (2) the negative electrons $\left(\mathrm{e}^{-}\right)$ move to the conductive band (CB), leaving the positive holes $\left(\mathrm{h^{+}}\right)$ in the valence band (VB); and (3) the reduction and oxidation reactions are taken place in the CB and VB, respectively. The degradation of pollutants is mainly achieved by oxidation reaction with the aid of active species. Therefore, the photodegradation of pollutants mainly depends on the amount of free radicals and holes involved in the reaction. However, some of the electron-hole pairs will recombine and the input energy will dissipate as heat or emitted light, leading to less free radicals and holes [54]. To enhance the photocatalytic activity of visible-light-driven catalyst, the absorption intensity to visible light should be increased and the recombination rate of the electron-hole pairs should be inhibited as much as possible. Therefore, the mechanism of how ISMIL-16 enhances photocatalytic degradation of TC was investigated from these two aspects.
The UV-vis absorption spectra was used to determinate the optical absorption properties of as-prepared samples. As showed in Fig. 8a, both MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ exhibited strong absorption ability to the visible light and their band gap absorption edges were around ${\sim}615~\mathrm{nm}$ and ${\sim}450~\mathrm{nm}$ , which were consistent with previous literature [44]. In addition, the absorption coefficients of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ composites were slightly less than those of pristine MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ (Fig. 8a and Fig. S2). These results indicated that the slight difference of visible-light absorption intensity did not affect the enhanced photocatalytic activity of ISMIL-16 for TC degradation.
PL emission spectra was used to indicate the recombination efficiency of photoexcited electron-hole pairs. Generally, a low PL intensity suggested a low recombination rate of photoexcited electron-hole pairs. From Fig. 9, the intensity of PL emission spectrum of MIL-100(Fe) was much higher than that of ISMIL-16. When the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ was added, the PL emission intensity decreased markedly, indicating that the photoexcited electron-hole pairs were efficiently separated, which may enhance the photocatalytic activity.
The three common types of heterojunctions in binary hybrid photocatalysts are shown in Fig. 10. For the type-Ⅰ heterojunction, compared with semiconductor B, the higher CB and lower VB of A will cause both electrons and holes to accumulate on the same semiconductor, resulting in higher recombination rate of electrons and holes. The electrons and holes in type-Ⅱ heterojunction with staggered gaps will respectively transfer to different semiconductors. The bandgaps of two semiconductors do not overlap with each other in type-Ⅲ heterojunction, hence the electrons and holes do not migrate between semiconductors, making no sense for the separation of electron-hole pairs. It is obvious that the photo-generated electron-hole pairs are efficiently separated in the catalysts with type-Ⅱ heterojunction, which is favorable to the photocatalytic activity.
To further identify the heterojunction type and charge transfer of ISMIL-16 hybrids, the band gap and band edge positions of the as-synthesized photocatalysts were estimated by calculation and drawing. In general, $\mathrm{E_{g}}$ of these samples can be estimated in the following equation :
$$
