It is well known that the existence of active species is a prerequisite for photodegradation of pollutants, and holes $\left(\mathrm{h^{+}}\right)$ , superoxide radicals $(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\bar{)}$ , and hydroxyl radicals (·OH) are the three major active species generated in the photocatalytic oxidation process [55]. To detect the active species involved in the photodegradation of TC over ISMIL-16, controlled radical-trapping experiments were carried out in the presence of different scavengers. It was reported that $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ , $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}$ , and ·OH were respectively trapped by sodium oxalate $\mathrm{(Na}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ , p-benzoquinone (BQ), and isopropyl alcohol (IPA) [56, 57]. As shown in Fig. 11a, the introduction of either $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ or BQ had a strong inhibitory effect on the degradation of TC, indicating that $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ and $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}$ played a dominant role in the photocatalytic process. Besides, the presence of IPA also decreased TC degradation rate, suggesting that ·OH was another contributor to TC photodegradation as well.
The ESR technology was used to further confirm the above radical-trapping results. As present in Fig. 11b and 11c, characteristic peaks of DMPO- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}$ and DMPO-·OH were observed when exposed to visible light, while no signal was detected in dark condition, suggesting the generations of $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}$ and ·OH after visible-light irradiation. Simultaneously, the ISMIL-16 exhibited higher peak strength compared with MIL-100(Fe) at the same irradiation time, suggesting more $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{\cdot}}$ and ·OH radicals were produced in the ISMIL-16 system. As shown in Fig. 11d, the intensities of $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ in the ISMIL-16 suspension were lower than that in the MIL-100(Fe), indicating more $\mathbf{h}^{+}$ generation onto the ISMIL-16. Furthermore, as the visible-light irradiation continued, the peak strengths decreased, demonstrating that $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ was constantly produced in the photocatalytic process. These results indicated that much more active species were generated in the ISMIL-16 system in comparison with pure MIL-100(Fe), which was the main reason for the enhanced photocatalytic activity of ISMIL-16.
Based on the above bandgaps analysis and trapping experimental results, the possible transfer behavior of electron-hole pairs and photodegradation process of TC for ISMIL-16 were shown in Fig. S3. Under visible light irradiation, both MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ could quickly produce electron-hole pairs due to their good visiblelight responsive characteristics and narrow bandgaps. Then the electrons and holes respectively accumulated in MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , owing to the constructed type- $\mathbb{I}$ heterojunction. The electrons could yield $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{^{-}}$ by combining with dissolved oxygen $(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , as the $\mathrm{E_{CB}}$ of MIL-100(Fe) is more negative than the $\mathrm{O}_{2}/{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ potential (- $0.33\mathrm{eV},$ ). Meanwhile, $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ could react with $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which was further transformed into ·OH [58, 59]. However, ·OH could not be formed in the VB of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ due to that the $\mathrm{{E}_{V B}}$ of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ is less positive than the $\mathrm{\cdotOH/H_{2}O}$ potential $\left(+2.40\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ [60]. Finally, TC molecules were degraded into small intermediates or directly into $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by these active species $\left(\mathbf{h}^{+},\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}\right.$ , and ·OH). These analyses were in good agreement with the radical-trapping and ESR results. The possible reactions for the photodegradation of TC were as follows:
