## 3.4. Potential application perspective
tests of ISMIL-16 for the TC removal.
In addition to photocatalytic activity, the stability and recyclability are also important for catalyst in practical applications. Further, the accumulation of large amounts of material may cause environmental and economic problems [61, 62]. The synthesized ISMIL-16 was recycled three times under the same conditions. The main procedures of this test were similar to that described in section 2.3, excepting that at the end of each cycle the photocatalysts were collected by filtration, washed with deionized water and ethanol for several times, and then dried in a vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the next cycle. As shown in Fig. 12, the ISMIL-16 sample displayed good stability and recyclability for the removal of TC during three cycles. The characteristic peaks of XRD patterns for ISMIL-16 before and after the photocatalytic reaction were almost same (Fig. 12a), indicating that the crystal structure remained stable after the reactions. Although TC removal efficiency decreased slightly after three consecutive cycles, $580\%$ of TC removal efficiency was still achieved (Fig. 12b). The slight decline may be due to the loss of photocatalyst dosage after each cycle. As a result, ISMIL-16 is very stable and recyclable for the removal of TC and the photocorrosion issue of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ also disappeared, which makes sense for practical application.
