## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a series of In2S3/MIL-100(Fe) heterojunction photocatalysts with different MIL-100(Fe) contents were successfully synthesized by a facile solvothermal method for the first time. Compared with single MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , the ISMIL-16 composites exhibited excellent photocatalytic activity for the removal of TC under visible-light irradiation, with the high removal efficiency $(88\%)$ and the low final TC concentration 1 $(1.2\mathrm{mg/L})$ ). The superior charge transfer and efficient electron-hole separation, benefiting from the formation of heterojunction between MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ , might be the main reasons for ISMIL-16 showing enhanced TC degradation performance. The radical-trapping experiments showed that the contributions to photocatalytic degradation were in the sequence of ${\bf h}^{+}>\cdot{\bf O}_{2}^{-}>$ ·OH. Besides, the effects of three common influencing factors on the removal of TC have been explored and it could be a reference for necessary optimizations in practical application. In addition, the ISMIL-16 sample was still stable in structure and photocatalytic activity, indicating its good stability and recyclability after three reaction cycles, which make sense for the practical industrial application. This study is a typical example about the utilization of semiconductor-MIL-100(Fe) as photocatalysts for the removal of TC from wastewater. As iron-based MOFs are cheap, low toxic, water stable, and strong visible-light responsive, more iron-based MOFs photocatalysts with various designs and modifications would be developed for the removal of refractory pollutants in the future.
